%N A399069 a(n) is the least positive integer divisible by at least n distinct entries of a single row of Pascal's triangle.
%C A399069 Let P_r = {binomial(r, k) : 0 <= k <= floor(r/2)}, the set of distinct entries in row r of Pascal's triangle. Then a(n) is the smallest LCM of n elements of some P_r.
%C A399069 For n >= 2, put U_n = LCM(1, 2, ..., 2*n - 1)/(2*n - 1). It suffices to consider rows r >= 2*n - 2 with binomial(r, n - 1) <= U_n, since any n distinct entries of row r include one that is at least binomial(r, n - 1).
%C A399069 If r_n is any row index at which a(n) is attained, then r_n = 2*n + O(n*exp(-c*(log(n))^(3/5)/(log(log(n)))^(1/5))) for some absolute constant c > 0; in particular, r_n ~ 2*n.
%H A399069 Felix Huber, <a href="/A399069/b399069.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..400</a>.
%H A399069 Bakir Farhi, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.2295">An identity involving the least common multiple of binomial coefficients and its application</a>, arXiv:0906.2295 [math.NT], 2009.
%H A399069 Bakir Farhi, <a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/40391228">An identity involving the least common multiple of binomial coefficients and its application</a>, Amer. Math. Monthly 116(9) (2009), 836-839.
%H A399069 Felix Huber, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.23024">Extremal Least Common Multiples in Rows of Pascal's Triangle</a>, arXiv:2609.23024 [math.CO], 2026.
%H A399069 Felix Huber, <a href="/A399069/a399069.txt">Python program</a>.
%F A399069 a(n) = min_{r >= 2*n - 2} min_{0 <= k_1 < ... < k_n <= floor(r/2)} LCM(binomial(r, k_1), ..., binomial(r, k_n)) for n >= 1.
%F A399069 binomial(2*n - 2, n - 1) <= a(n) <= A002944(2*n - 1) for n >= 1.
%F A399069 log(a(n)) = 2*n + O(n*exp(-c*(log(n))^(3/5)/(log(log(n)))^(1/5))) for some absolute constant c > 0.
%F A399069 Limit_{n -> infinity} a(n)^(1/n) = e^2.
%e A399069 a(3) = 10: row 5 has distinct entries 1, 5, 10, whose LCM is 10. No smaller value is possible: row 4 gives LCM 12, while for r >= 6 any three distinct entries include one at least binomial(r, 2) >= 15.
%e A399069 a(15) = 72382733280 is attained in row 34 by selecting binomial(34, k) for k = 0, 1, ..., 7, 9, 10, ..., 15. This is the first n for which no optimal set consists of the first n distinct entries of a Pascal row.
%o A399069 (Python) # See Huber link.
